From: klizas <lukas@klizas.lt>
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: macsmc: use BCLM for the charge limit on T2

T2 firmware has CHLS and BCLM and enforces both, against different
figures: CHLS against BUIC, the UI charge macOS shows, and BCLM against
BRSC, the gauge's relative charge that CAPACITY reports on ACPI. BUIC
runs ahead of BRSC. MacBookPro16,1, charging on AC:

  BRSC  76  77  78  79  80
  BUIC  80  81  82  83  84

The driver prefers CHLS when it exists, so an end threshold of 80 stops
charging at a CAPACITY of 76. With CHLS at 100 and BCLM at 80, charging
ran on and stopped at BRSC 80 (BUIC 84), as applesmc-t2 did with BCLM.
Nothing on 7.3 writes BCLM either, so a BCLM left by applesmc-t2 caps
every charge, including one asked to go to 100.

On ACPI, use BCLM when it exists. CHLS keeps its last value: set it to
100 once before switching, or it stays a second, lower limit.

Applies on top of 0001-macsmc-power-design-capacity.patch.

--- a/drivers/power/supply/macsmc-power.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/macsmc-power.c
@@ -1113,7 +1113,12 @@
 			props[nprops++] = POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_ENERGY_FULL;
 			props[nprops++] = POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_ENERGY_NOW;
 
-			if (apple_smc_key_exists(smc, SMC_KEY(CHLS)))
+			/*
+			 * T2 applies CHLS to BUIC (the UI charge) but BCLM to
+			 * BRSC, which CAPACITY reports.
+			 */
+			if (apple_smc_key_exists(smc, SMC_KEY(CHLS)) &&
+			    !apple_smc_key_exists(smc, SMC_KEY(BCLM)))
 				power->has_chls = true;
 			if (power->has_chls || apple_smc_key_exists(smc, SMC_KEY(BCLM)))
 				props[nprops++] = POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CHARGE_CONTROL_END_THRESHOLD;
